    class MultiVerifier:
        """Cross-verifies an endpoint's location and nature with many independent
    methods, then fuses them into a consensus + confidence + conflict report.

    Design principle: no single source is trusted. Accuracy comes from
    *agreement* across independent methods; disagreement is surfaced and is
    itself a strong VPN / proxy / anycast / relocation indicator. The verifier
    never overclaims — it reports a confidence grade and always shows its work.

    Methods fused:
      1. GeoIP primary (local MaxMind DB or ip-api)   → country/city vote
      2. GeoIP alternate (ipwho.is)                    → independent vote
      3. Reverse DNS (IATA/city codes, ccTLD)          → independent vote
      4. RDAP registry country                         → independent vote
      5. RIPEstat + Cymru DNS-ASN (two BGP sources)    → third geo vote + origin AS x2
      6. RTT latency band (active ping)                → physical plausibility
      7. TCP-RTT divergence (connect timing vs ICMP)   → relay/edge-termination proof
      8. TTL / OS fingerprint (active ping)            → OS + hop distance
      9. HOPS last-mile geolocation (TTL-limited ping → where the path lands
         + PTR/IATA fallback when GeoIP is silent on the hop)
     10. VPN/proxy TCP port fingerprint (active)       → service evidence
     11. Infrastructure classification (org/ASN/CDN)   → VPN/proxy/hosting flag
     12. DNS leak / resolver mismatch (rDNS TLD)       → VPN DNS-leak signal
     13. TLS JA3 fingerprint (active, port 443)        → client software ID
     14. TLS server certificate (subject/SAN/issuer)   → real operator identity
     15. TCP MSS clamping (sniffed SYN options)        → tunnel on the path
     16. Path-MTU ladder (DF pings, 1500→1300)         → encapsulation bytes
     17. Passive TCP fingerprint (win/opts/TTL)        → OS + TCP/ICMP path split
     18. TUNX MSS×PMTU localization                    → whose tunnel it is
     19. EDGE HTTP headers (Via / CF-RAY / CF-PoP)     → in-path proxy + PoP
     20. ASN correlation (known VPN/proxy ASNs)        → VPN provider match
     21. LocationVerifier fused cross-check            → independent grade
     22. PATH plausibility (RTT + hop count vs claim)  → relay/exit-node proof
     23. PEER coherence sweep (same-city RTT outliers) → relabelled exits
     24. IKE SA_INIT probe (udp 500/4500)              → IPsec gateway proof
     25. PROX live SOCKS/HTTP-CONNECT negotiation      → proven relay
     26. TLSALT alt-port cert grab (1194/8443/…)       → VPN cert identity
     27. BANNER service banners (SSH/SMTP/FTP/…)       → 3rd OS + hostname
     28. TOREXIT TorDNSEL exit-list DNS query          → official Tor egress
     29. TSCLK TCP-timestamp clock (sniffed SYN-ACKs)  → multiplexed stacks
     30. IPID IP header ID streams (sniffed, passive)  → shared egress
     31. TTLX TCP-vs-ICMP hop split (sniffed TTL)      → split termination
    post-consensus:
     32. LAND last-hop-vs-consensus cross-check          → path lands elsewhere
     33. SRVLOC fused serving location (EDGE/last-mile)  → where it really is
    cross-cutting (registry/passive/service layers):
     34. GEOFEED RFC 9092 operator-declared location      → the owner's own claim
     35. RPKI route-origin validation (RIPEstat)         → hijack/rogue prefix
     36. TSRTT passive TCP-handshake RTT (sniffed)       → real RTT, no ICMP needed
     37. H2FP HTTP/2 SETTINGS fingerprint (ALPN h2)      → service-stack identity
     38. RDRCT redirect chase (1 hop) + landing edge      → where it really sends you
    silent-tunnel / non-IP-channel layer (wire_hint = passive sniffer
    harvest; the protocols that answer NO probe can only be seen here):
     39. WGWIRE WireGuard handshake/transport signature   → unprobeable VPN proof
     40. OVPNOP OpenVPN reset/control opcodes on-wire     → session-setup proof
     41. DTLSOP DTLS records on UDP                       → AnyConnect-class VPN
     42. L3TUN ESP/AH/GRE/IP-in-IP/6to4 non-port protos   → tunnels no scan sees
     43. ICMPTUN oversized/high-entropy ICMP payloads     → ping data channel
     44. QUICV QUIC Version-Negotiation probe (udp/443)   → HTTP/3·MASQUE proof
     45. ENCF header-less high-entropy payloads           → SS/V2Ray/obfs4 shape
     46. TLSW TLS records on non-standard ports           → covert TLS channel
     47. WSTUN WebSocket upgrade on the flow              → HTTP-masked tunnel
     48. ECH Encrypted-ClientHello marker                 → real SNI hidden
     49. SNIFX SNI absent from local DNS answers          → domain-fronting sign
     50. ONEF one-directional flow (send/receive-only)    → one-way channel
     51. TUN6 Teredo IPv6-over-UDP/3544 transition tunnel → non-IP channel
    coverage-expansion layer:
     52. MPTCP multipath-TCP option on the wire           → bonding VPN
     53. STUN STUN/TURN + mapped-addr decode              → real egress IP
     54. OVRLY VXLAN/Geneve overlay encap                 → cloud mask
     55. L2TPW L2TP frames on-wire                        → access VPN
     56. NATT IPsec NAT-T + ESP-in-UDP                    → IPsec data channel
     57. OVTCP OpenVPN-over-TCP framing                   → stealth VPN
     58. PROXW SOCKS5/CONNECT verbs on-wire               → relayed flow
     59. EDNS DoT/DoH/DoQ encrypted DNS                   → resolver off :53
     60. TORCLI Tor directory-authority contact           → local Tor client
     61. V6CH IPv6 dual-stack endpoint                    → IPv4-bypass path
     62. SVCVAL protocol-native proof on claimed ports    → TCP-accept≠service
     63. ACCALL indiscriminate TCP-accept edge            → L4 relay
    decapsulation layer:
     64. INNER decapsulated IPIP/GRE/GTP-U/ICMP contents  → real pre-tunnel IPs
     65. GTPW GTP mobile-core tunnel (2123/2152/3386)     → carrier mask
     66. L3CHAN SCTP/DCCP/RSVP/UDPLite non-port transport → portless channel
     67. FRAGW fragment-only flow                         → evasion shape
     68. HOSTX Host-header vs SNI/DNS divergence on CDN   → fronting proven
    evasion layer:
     69. DNSTUN tunnel-shaped queries to a public :53     → resolver is a tunnel
     70. PORTW non-DNS on :53 / plaintext on TLS port     → port costume
     71. EXT6 IPv6 extension-header chains                → header stuffing
     72. P2PM UDP hole-punch mesh fan-out                 → overlay/P2P proxy
     73. FFLUX domain rotating through many IPs           → botnet churn
     74. CGN carrier-grade NAT range                      → shared ISP mask
    quote/route layer:
     75. ICMQ ICMP error quotes embed the dropped packet  → attempted flows
     76. REDIR ICMP redirect on the wire                  → route manipulation
     77. IPOPT IPv4 options (RR/TS/LSRR/…) on-wire        → header channels
     78. QROT same QUIC conn-ID from a second address     → rotated alias
     79. REBIND one name answers public+private           → rebinding mask
    session-link layer:
     80. SALINK ESP SPI / WG index / IKE cookie reused     → one SA, two IPs
     81. DNSEX oversized DNS answers (TXT/blob)           → inbound tunnel data
     82. KNOCK many distinct ports SYNed in a burst       → knock-server
     83. KALV repeated sub-16B UDP payloads               → keepalive cadence
    unmask layer:
     84. V6DEC Teredo/6to4/NAT64/ISATAP/v4-mapped decode   → address self-unmasks
     85. PROXYH PROXY-protocol v1/v2 header on the wire    → relay names client
     86. XFF X-Forwarded-For/Forwarded header chain        → proxy names client
     87. QSHOR short-header QUIC DCID post-handshake       → migration linking
     88. PTRX PTR hostname fails to resolve back           → borrowed rDNS name
    lan-hijack/leak layer:
     89. SIPV SIP Via/Contact/SDP inner-address leak        → VoIP pre-NAT addr
     90. FTPP FTP PORT/EPRT/PASV embedded endpoint          → data-channel addr
     91. TTLV disjoint TTL families from one remote         → multipath/spoof pool
    theoretical-coverage layer:
     92. NEST inner packet is itself a tunnel protocol        → stacked relays
     93. IPIDLNK shared kernel IP-ID counter across addresses → one stack, two IPs
     94. RSTINJ RST on a foreign TTL family                   → on-path killer
     95. TSLINK shared TCP timestamp clock across addresses   → shared kernel
     96. ONIONQ .onion/.i2p lookups at a public resolver      → overlay probes
     97. NEAR public remote ≤3 hops away (TTL ≥252)            → claims far, is near
     98. LOWTTL TTL ≤3 on an established flow                  → crafted to expire here
     99. XFLAGS NULL/Xmas/SYN+FIN flag combos                  → scan/covert bits
     100. RH0 IPv6 routing-header type 0 (banned source route) → path injection
     101. P0 port 0 in a flow                                  → malformed/covert
     102. ICMPR rare ICMP control types (13-18) on a flow      → router recon
     103. CERTX cert commonNames cover none of the SNI names   → TLS-intercept shape
     — monitor-level: RAWETH non-IP ethertype channels, MARTIAN
       same-MAC multi-IP spoofs, TTLINJ routed LAN-TTL
       injections, RDHCP(+v6) rogue DHCP/DHCPv6 servers, L2SPOOF
       ARP/NDP poison pairs, ARPSTORM gratuitous floods, ROGRA
       rogue router adverts, QINQ double-VLAN tunnels, and
       LLMNS name-service poisoners emit bounded deductions.
     — the L3 family now covers IPv6 next-headers (ESP/GRE/IPIP on
       v6) and ICMPv6 quote/tunnel/redirect; V6DEC also names
       Yggdrasil (0200::/7) and CJDNS (fc00::/8) overlay meshes;
       PROXW classifies SOCKS4/4a requests.
     — traceroute anomalies (country backtrack, VPN-labelled hops) also feed
       back into the destination's report as TRACE rows after a TRC run.
    """

        def __init__(self, geoip=None, location_verifier=None, proxy_detector=None,
                     local_geo_getter=None):
            self._geoip = geoip
            self._lv = location_verifier
            self._proxy = proxy_detector
            # Callable returning {'lat': .., 'lon': ..} for THIS machine — used by
            # the PATH plausibility method (RTT vs claimed-distance contradiction).
            self._local_geo_getter = local_geo_getter
            self._cache: dict[str, dict] = {}
            self._lock = threading.Lock()
            # PEER sweep re-correlates every cached report — debounce so it runs
            # periodically, not once per verified IP (O(ips × cache) blowup).
            self._last_coherence_sweep = 0.0

        # ---------- public ----------
        def verify(self, ip: str, active: bool = True, port_timeout: float = 0.6,
                   mss_hint=None, tcpfp_hint=None, tsclk_hint=None,
                   ipid_hint=None, tsrtt_hint=None, wire_hint=None) -> dict:
            with self._lock:
                cached = self._cache.get(ip)
            if cached:
                return cached

            report = {
                'ip': ip, 'methods': [], 'consensus_country': '', 'consensus_city': '',
                'confidence': 0, 'grade': 'UNVERIFIED', 'conflicts': [],
                'agree': 0, 'total_votes': 0, 'lat': 0, 'lon': 0,
                'vpn_score': 0, 'is_vpn': False, 'is_proxy': False,
                'is_hosting': False, 'is_cdn': False, 'labels': [],
                'ttl': None, 'ttl_os': '', 'hop_distance': None, 'rtt_ms': None,
                'rtt_jitter_ms': None, 'mss': None, 'path_mtu': None,
                'tcpfp_os': '', 'tcpfp_win': 0, 'tcpfp_opts': '',
                'tcp_hops': None,
                'tsclk_hz': None, 'tsclk_uptime_s': None,
                'tsclk_streams': 0, 'tsclk_mux': False, 'is_tor': False,
                'ipid_kind': '', 'ipid_streams': 0,
                'ike_responder': False, 'ike_vendor': '', 'ike_detail': '',
                'proxy_state': '', 'proxy_detail': '',
                'tlsalt_port': 0, 'tlsalt_cn': '', 'tlsalt_org': '',
                'banner_ports': [], 'banner_os': '', 'banner_host': '',
                'banner_kex': '', 'tsrtt_ms': None,
                'geofeed_cc': '', 'geofeed_city': '', 'rpki_state': '',
                'h2_alpn': '', 'h2_sig': '', 'rdrct': '',
                'wire_wg': 0, 'wire_ovpn': '', 'wire_dtls': 0,
                'wire_l3': {}, 'icmp_tun': 0, 'enc_flows': 0,
                'tls_weird': 0, 'wstun': 0, 'ech_seen': False,
                'no_sni': 0, 'sni_unresolved': [], 'fronting': False,
                'quic': False, 'quic_versions': [], 'one_way': '',
                'mptcp': 0, 'stun_cnt': 0, 'stun_mapped': '', 'stun_leak': False,
                'vxlan': 0, 'geneve': 0, 'l2tp': 0, 'natt': 0, 'espudp': 0,
                'zt': 0, 'tailscale': 0, 'nebula': 0, 'zt_node': None,
                'ovpntcp': 0, 'proxy_chan': 0, 'edns_dot': 0, 'edns_doh': '',
                'edns_doq': 0, 'torda': False, 'v6_chan': False,
                'gtp': 0, 'l3chan': {}, 'inner_eps': [], 'inner_public': [],
                'frag': 0, 'frag_heavy': False, 'host_front': '',
                'dnstun': 0, 'dnstun_q': '', 'portwear': 0, 'tls_invert': 0,
                'ext6': 0, 'p2p': 0, 'fflux': '', 'cgn': False,
                'icmq': {}, 'icmpt': {}, 'ipopt': 0, 'qrot': '', 'rebind': '',
                'nest': 0, 'ipidlink': '', 'rstinj': 0, 'tslink': '',
                'tsfreq': 0.0, 'tsflink': '',
                'horder': '', 'hstack': '', 'hlink': '',
                'srvban': '', 'srvx': '', 'srvvia': '', 'srvlink': '',
                'sshver': '', 'hassh': '', 'hassh_tag': '', 'hasshlink': '',
                'sshkey': '', 'sshkey_alg': '', 'sshkeylink': '',
                'passban': '', 'echoalpha': '', 'tflink': '',
                'onionq': 0, 'xflags': 0, 'near': 0, 'lowttl': 0,
                'rh0': 0, 'p0': 0, 'icmpr': {}, 'cert_cn': '', 'certx': 0,
                'salink': '', 'dnsex': 0, 'dnsex_t': '', 'knock': 0,
                'kalv': 0, 'ptrx': '', 'v6dec': '', 'v6dec_all': [],
                'proxyh': 0, 'proxyh_ips': [], 'xff': '', 'qshor': 0,
                'sipv': '', 'sipv_ips': [], 'ftpp': '', 'ftpp_ips': [],
                'ttlv': '',
                'exit_provider': '', 'exit_kind': '', 'exit_host': '',
                'exit_city': '', 'exit_cc': '', 'apple_relay': '',
                'apple_city': '', 'apple_cc': '', 'apple_region': '',
                'onionoo': None, 'dnsbl': [], 'crtsh': None,
                'ident': {}, 'chaos': {}, 'stun_srv': {},
                'ike1': {}, 'portal': [], 'hopdelta': {},
                'smbid': None, 'icmpts': None,
                'ldapid': None, 'rtspid': None, 'rdpid': None,
                'svcid': None, 'krbrealm': None,
                'dbid': None, 'sipid': None, 'mqttid': None,
                'mbusid': None, 'bacnet': None,
                'h2fp': None, 'btcid': None, 'dnp3': None,
                'ipmip': None, 'rpcb': None, 'pjlid': None,
                'ircid': None, 'telban': None, 'ovmgmt': None,
                'zabbid': None, 'rsyncid': None, 'amqpid': None,
                'kafkaid': None, 'x11id': None, 'bgpid': None,
                'httpid': None, 'mcid': None, 'winrm': None,
                'pptpid': None, 'sstpid': None, 'i2pid': None,
                'l2tid': None, 'enip': None, 'ovudp': None,
                'natpmp': None, 'mumble': None, 'cassid': None,
                'vncid': None, 'coapid': None, 'ts3id': None,
                'ftpid': None, 'mailid': None, 'xmppid': None,
                'orcid': None, 'db2id': None, 'chid': None,
                'opcua': None, 'vaultid': None, 'afpid': None,
                'identd': None, 'gitid': None, 'wsdid': None,
                'iec104': None, 'iscsiid': None, 'nutid': None,
                's7id': None, 'promid': None, 'dockid': None,
                'dicomid': None, 'atgid': None, 'ikev2': None,
                'srcds': None, 'mdnsid': None, 'vmwid': None,
                'diamid': None, 'natsid': None, 'q3id': None,
                'mcbed': None, 'checkmk': None, 'radius': None,
                'svnid': None, 'amiid': None, 'tincid': None,
                'epmdid': None, 'jdwpid': None, 'plexid': None,
                'adbid': None, 'splitr': 0, 'exfil': 0,
                'txidseq': 0, 'dscpfld': 0, 'deaddrop': '',
                'synpay': 0, 'unsl': 0, 'rtpm': 0, 'isnf': 0,
                'winfld': 0, 'tsecf': 0, 'dga_dom': 0,
                'rstpay': 0, 'ipidf': 0, 'icmidf': 0,
                'deaddrop_lo': '', 'wpadq': 0, 'wpada': '',
                'pacget': '', 'ecnf': 0, 'ecs_subnet': '',
                'aliases': [],
                'wire_seslink': '', 'wire_flowlbl': 0, 'wire_eui64': '',
                'wire_eui64_vendor': '', 'wire_sctp': 0, 'wire_sctp_ips': [],
                'wire_becon': None, 'wire_psize': None, 'wire_tcpao': '',
                'wire_urg': 0, 'wire_l2link': '',
                'certlink': '', 'flowcorr': '',
                'accept_all_ports': [], 'svc_confirmed': [],
                'serving_city': '', 'serving_cc': '', 'serving_src': '',
                'open_ports': [], 'org': '', 'isp': '', 'asn': '', 'rdns': '',
                'dns_leak': None, 'tls_ja3': None,
                'tcp_rtt_ms': None, 'tls_cert_cn': '', 'tls_cert_org': '',
                'tls_cert_sans': [], 'last_mile': [],
                'http_server': '', 'http_via': '',
                'edge_city': '', 'edge_cc': '', 'edge_iata': '',
                'ripe_prefix': '', 'ripe_asn': '',
                'path_claimed_km': None, 'path_ceiling_km': None,
                'path_implausible': False,
                'summary': '', 'loc_proof': [],
            }
            try:
                addr = ipaddress.ip_address(ip)
            except ValueError:
                report['summary'] = 'invalid IP address'
                report['grade'] = 'N/A'
                return report
            if not addr.is_global:
                if addr.version == 4 and addr in _CGN_NET:
                    # CGN — shared ISP infrastructure: the "endpoint" is the
                    # carrier's mask, not a single customer's real address.
                    report['cgn'] = True
                    report['summary'] = ('carrier-grade NAT shared ISP '
                                         'address (100.64.0.0/10) — the '
                                         'carrier mask, not the customer')
                    report['labels'].append(
                        'CGN shared-ISP address (carrier mask)')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'CGN', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'info',
                         'detail': 'carrier-grade NAT range 100.64.0.0/10 — '
                                   'shared ISP infrastructure address'})
                else:
                    report['summary'] = ('private / LAN address — not '
                                         'externally verifiable')
                report['grade'] = 'N/A'
                return report

            cc_votes: dict[str, list] = {}   # country code -> [method names]
            city_votes: dict[str, list] = {}

            def cast(name, cc, city, detail):
                cc = (cc or '').upper()[:2]
                city = (city or '').strip()
                m = {'name': name, 'cc': cc, 'city': city, 'detail': detail, 'status': 'info'}
                report['methods'].append(m)
                if cc:
                    cc_votes.setdefault(cc, []).append(name)
                if city and len(city) > 2:
                    city_votes.setdefault(city.lower(), []).append(name)
                return m

            # --- 1) GeoIP primary ---
            geo = {}
            if self._geoip is not None:
                try:
                    geo = self._geoip.get_full(ip) or {}
                except Exception:
                    geo = {}
            if geo:
                report['lat'] = geo.get('lat', 0) or 0
                report['lon'] = geo.get('lon', 0) or 0
                report['org'] = geo.get('org', '') or ''
                report['isp'] = geo.get('isp', '') or ''
                report['asn'] = geo.get('as', '') or ''
                pcc = geo.get('countryCode') or geo.get('country') or ''
                cast('GeoIP', pcc, geo.get('city', ''),
                     f"{geo.get('city','?')}, {geo.get('country','?')} · {geo.get('org','?')}"
                     f" [{geo.get('_source','?')}]")

            # ---------- probe wave: every independent network method runs in
            # parallel — each probe carries its own hard timeout, so the whole
            # wave costs ~max(probe) instead of the ~20s serial sum. Method rows
            # are still emitted below in their documented order. ----------
            probes: dict = {}

            def _job(name, fn):
                try:
                    probes[name] = fn(ip)
                except Exception:
                    probes[name] = None

            def _ptrx_job(i):
                # Forward-confirmed reverse DNS — the PTR hostname's own
                # A/AAAA answers must contain the endpoint, else the name
                # is borrowed/forged (a spoofed-identity mask).
                rd = None
                for _ in range(50):
                    if 'rdns' in probes:
                        rd = probes['rdns']
                        break
                    time.sleep(0.1)
                host = rd[2] if rd else ''
                if not host:
                    return None
                addrs = _fwd_lookup_bounded(host)
                if not addrs:
                    return ('unresolvable', host)
                return ('match' if i in addrs else 'mismatch', host)

            def _crtsh_job(i):
                # Waits for the rDNS probe inside the wave — the cert-log
                # search is keyed on the PTR hostname the endpoint claims.
                rd = None
                for _ in range(50):
                    if 'rdns' in probes:
                        rd = probes['rdns']
                        break
                    time.sleep(0.1)
                return self._crtsh_query(i, (rd[2] if rd else '') or '')

            def _exit_job(i):
                # Provider-published exit lists + Apple relay egress map.
                # Warm the disk cache synchronously; if the feeds are stale
                # or absent, kick a background fetch and answer with what
                # we have — the first verifications of a run may miss, the
                # thousands after it hit the cache.
                _exit_feed_load_disk()
                if not _exit_feed_cache['ts'] or \
                        time.time() - _exit_feed_cache['ts'] >= _EXIT_FEED_TTL_S:
                    threading.Thread(target=_fetch_exit_feeds,
                                     daemon=True).start()
                return _exit_lookup(i)

            jobs = {
                'alt_geoip': self._alt_geoip,
                'rdns': self._rdns_country,
                'ptrx': _ptrx_job,
                'rdap': self._rdap_country,
                'ripe': self._ripe_lookup,
                'cymru': self._cymru_asn,
                # Tor exit list — queries OUR resolver, never the endpoint, so
                # it runs even when active probing is disabled.
                'tor': self._tor_dnsel,
                # Geofeed — the network OPERATOR's own declared location for the
                # prefix (RFC 9092). Registry-class: asks the RIR + the feed
                # host, never the endpoint.
                'geofeed': self._geofeed_lookup,
                # Provider-published exit lists — the operators' own records.
                'exit': _exit_job,
                # Onionoo — Tor's official relay directory (guard/middle too).
                'onionoo': self._tor_onionoo,
                # Abuse blocklists — shared/anonymising infrastructure by
                # community reputation. Queries OUR resolver only.
                'dnsbl': self._dnsbl_check,
                # crt.sh — certificate transparency: every logged cert for
                # the endpoint's claimed rDNS domain.
                'crtsh': _crtsh_job,
            }
            if active:
                def _lastmile_job(i):
                    # Waits for the TTL fingerprint inside the wave (the hop
                    # distance aims the pings), then fires the 3 aimed probes.
                    fp = None
                    for _ in range(50):
                        if 'ttl' in probes:
                            fp = probes['ttl']
                            break
                        time.sleep(0.1)
                    return self._lastmile_pings(i, (fp or {}).get('hops'))

                def _wait_ports(i, fn):
                    # Waits for the VPN-port scan inside the wave, then runs
                    # protocol verification against whatever it found open.
                    for _ in range(50):
                        if 'ports' in probes:
                            return fn(i, probes['ports'] or [])
                        time.sleep(0.1)
                    return fn(i, [])

                jobs.update({
                    'rtt': self._rtt_measure,
                    'ttl': self._ttl_fingerprint,
                    'tcp443': lambda i: self._tcp_rtt(i, 443),
                    'tcp80': lambda i: self._tcp_rtt(i, 80),
                    'ports': lambda i: self._scan_vpn_ports(i, port_timeout),
                    'tls': self._tls_ja3_fingerprint,
                    'lastmile': _lastmile_job,
                    'pmtu': self._path_mtu,
                    'http': lambda i: self._http_banner(i),
                    # IKE SA_INIT to UDP 500/4500 — a formatted reply means the
                    # endpoint itself speaks IPsec: a VPN gateway proven by its
                    # own protocol, not by port guessing.
                    'ike': self._ike_probe,
                    # Banner read on SSH/FTP/SMTP/POP3/IMAP — a third OS-class
                    # signal plus real hostnames the rDNS may not carry.
                    'banner': self._banner_grab,
                    # Actually negotiate SOCKS4/SOCKS5 + HTTP-CONNECT on the
                    # flagged proxy ports — upgrades "port open" to "proven relay".
                    'prox': lambda i: _wait_ports(i, self._proxy_verify),
                    # TLS cert grab on alt VPN ports (OpenVPN-TCP 1194 etc.) —
                    # the cert names the operator even when :443 is closed.
                    'tlsalt': lambda i: _wait_ports(i, self._tls_alt),
                    # Protocol-native handshake on every claimed-open fingerprint
                    # port — TCP-accept proves nothing on a transparent edge.
                    'svcval': lambda i: _wait_ports(i, self._svc_validate),
                    # QUIC Version-Negotiation probe on udp/443 — a formatted
                    # VN reply proves HTTP/3: the MASQUE-class transport modern
                    # encrypted relays hide behind "it's just HTTPS".
                    'quic': self._quic_probe,
                    # Self-naming services — NBNS/SNMP/NTP/SSDP/SSRP, five
                    # datagrams that make the endpoint state its own name.
                    'ident': self._identity_probe,
                    # DNS CHAOS — hostname.bind/version.bind + open-recursion.
                    'chaos': self._dns_chaos_probe,
                    # STUN/TURN binding — a reflexive relay names its stack.
                    'stunp': self._stun_probe,
                    # IKEv1 aggressive — legacy IPsec answers what IKEv2
                    # ignores, and declares its own ID payload in cleartext.
                    'ike1': self._ike1_probe,
                    # VPN-portal fingerprint — SSTP/AnyConnect/Forti/
                    # GlobalProtect/OpenVPN-AS/Pulse pages behind :443.
                    'portal': self._portal_probe,
                    # SMB NTLMSSP — the endpoint hands over its own
                    # NetBIOS/DNS names + clock before any auth.
                    'smbid': self._smb_probe,
                    # ICMP timestamp — the endpoint's own clock (and often
                    # its local-time UTC offset) in one echo.
                    'icmpts': self._icmp_ts_probe,
                    # LDAP rootDSE — directory servers name their own
                    # domain/hostname in cleartext on anonymous query.
                    'ldapid': self._ldap_probe,
                    # RTSP OPTIONS — camera/NVR class devices name their
                    # vendor+firmware in the Server: banner.
                    'rtspid': self._rtsp_probe,
                    # X.224/RDP negotiation — RDP hosts declare their own
                    # security stack (and NLA mode) on a 19-byte probe.
                    'rdpid': self._rdp_probe,
                    # Unauthenticated self-naming services — exposed ES /
                    # Docker / Redis / Memcached / K8s / ZK / Couch /
                    # Prometheus name their own deployment in one query.
                    'svcid': self._svc_ident_probe,
                    # Kerberos KRB_ERROR — the domain controller answers a
                    # bogus AS-REQ with its REAL realm in cleartext.
                    'krbrealm': self._krb_probe,
                    # DBID — MySQL/PG/MSSQL greet with version+build
                    # before any auth exists.
                    'dbid': self._dbid_probe,
                    # SIPID — PBX/SBC answers OPTIONS with Server banner.
                    'sipid': self._sipid_probe,
                    # MQTTID — broker CONNACK on 1883.
                    'mqttid': self._mqtt_probe,
                    # MBUSID — Modbus FC43 device identification.
                    'mbusid': self._mbus_probe,
                    # BACNET — Who-Is → I-Am device instance+vendor.
                    'bacnet': self._bacnet_probe,
                    # H2FP — h2 SETTINGS fingerprint via ALPN.
                    'h2fp': self._h2fp_probe,
                    # BTCID — crypto node version handshake.
                    'btcid': self._btc_probe,
                    # DNP3 — SCADA link-layer status.
                    'dnp3': self._dnp3_probe,
                    # IPMIP — BMC RMCP ping (out-of-band hardware).
                    'ipmip': self._ipmi_probe,
                    # RPCB — rpcbind dump: Unix service inventory.
                    'rpcb': self._rpcb_probe,
                    # PJLID — printer self-ID on 9100.
                    'pjlid': self._pjl_probe,
                    # IRCID — IRC 001 welcome names server/network.
                    'ircid': self._irc_probe,
                    # TELBAN — telnet banner w/ IAC strip (CPE/router).
                    'telban': self._tel_probe,
                    # OVMGMT — OpenVPN mgmt interface names the daemon.
                    'ovmgmt': self._ovmgmt_probe,
                    # ZABBID — zabbix agent leaks system.hostname.
                    'zabbid': self._zabbix_probe,
                    # RSYNCID — @RSYNCD greeting + module list.
                    'rsyncid': self._rsync_probe,
                    # AMQPID — connection.start server properties.
                    'amqpid': self._amqp_probe,
                    # KAFKAID — ApiVersions broker table.
                    'kafkaid': self._kafka_probe,
                    # X11ID — X server vendor / auth reason.
                    'x11id': self._x11_probe,
                    # BGPID — OPEN reply names ASN + router-id.
                    'bgpid': self._bgp_probe,
                    # HTTPID — generic web identity on the open ports.
                    'httpid': self._http_probe,
                    # MCID — minecraft status ping names the server.
                    'mcid': self._mc_probe,
                    # WINRM — Microsoft-HTTPAPI proves Windows.
                    'winrm': self._winrm_probe,
                    # PPTPID — SCCRP host/vendor fields.
                    'pptpid': self._pptp_probe,
                    # SSTPID — RRAS SSTP URL answer.
                    'sstpid': self._sstp_probe,
                    # I2PID — SAM bridge HELLO names the router.
                    'i2pid': self._i2p_probe,
                    # L2TID — SCCRP AVP host/vendor names.
                    'l2tid': self._l2tp_probe,
                    # ENIP — EtherNet/IP ListIdentity self-ID.
                    'enip': self._enip_probe,
                    # OVUDP — OpenVPN UDP hard-reset reply.
                    'ovudp': self._ovudp_probe,
                    # NATPMP — gateway's real WAN IP.
                    'natpmp': self._natpmp_probe,
                    # MUMBLE — voice server version ping.
                    'mumble': self._mumble_probe,
                    # CASSID — Cassandra CQL SUPPORTED map.
                    'cassid': self._cass_probe,
                    # VNCID — RFB greeting version.
                    'vncid': self._vnc_probe,
                    # COAPID — /.well-known/core resource map.
                    'coapid': self._coap_probe,
                    # TS3ID — TeamSpeak ServerQuery banner.
                    'ts3id': self._ts3_probe,
                    # FTPID — SYST OS class + daemon banner.
                    'ftpid': self._ftp_probe,
                    # MAILID — IMAP/POP3/SMTP capability sets.
                    'mailid': self._mail_probe,
                    # XMPPID — stream 'from' domain leak.
                    'xmppid': self._xmpp_probe,
                    # ORCID — TNS COMMAND=version.
                    'orcid': self._orc_probe,
                    # DB2ID — DRDA EXCSAT server name.
                    'db2id': self._db2_probe,
                    # CHID — ClickHouse native hello.
                    'chid': self._chid_probe,
                    # OPCUA — HEL→ACK industrial gateway.
                    'opcua': self._opcua_probe,
                    # VAULTID — /v1/sys/health secrets-manager.
                    'vaultid': self._vault_probe,
                    # AFPID — DSI GetStatus server name.
                    'afpid': self._afp_probe,
                    # IDENTD — auth service OS+user leak.
                    'identd': self._identd_probe,
                    # GITID — git-daemon ref advertisement.
                    'gitid': self._git_probe,
                    # WSDID — WS-Discovery device XAddrs.
                    'wsdid': self._wsd_probe,
                    # IEC104 — SCADA telecontrol STARTDT.
                    'iec104': self._iec104_probe,
                    # ISCSIID — iSCSI target name.
                    'iscsiid': self._iscsi_probe,
                    # NUTID — UPS daemon self-ID.
                    'nutid': self._nut_probe,
                    # S7ID — Siemens PLC module ID.
                    's7id': self._s7_probe,
                    # PROMID — node_exporter metrics hostname.
                    'promid': self._prom_probe,
                    # DOCKID — Docker daemon API version.
                    'dockid': self._dock_probe,
                    # DICOMID — PACS implementation name.
                    'dicomid': self._dicom_probe,
                    # ATGID — fuel tank-gauge inventory.
                    'atgid': self._atg_probe,
                    # IKEV2 — modern IPsec SA_INIT responder.
                    'ikev2': self._ikev2_probe,
                    # SRCDS — game server self-description.
                    'srcds': self._srcds_probe,
                    # MDNSID — mDNS service inventory.
                    'mdnsid': self._mdns_probe,
                    # VMWID — hypervisor auth banner.
                    'vmwid': self._vmw_probe,
                    # DIAMID — Diameter AAA Origin-Host.
                    'diamid': self._diam_probe,
                    # NATSID — NATS INFO banner.
                    'natsid': self._nats_probe,
                    # Q3ID — Quake3-engine getstatus.
                    'q3id': self._q3_probe,
                    # MCBED — Bedrock RakNet pong.
                    'mcbed': self._mcbed_probe,
                    # CHECKMK — agent header names the host.
                    'checkmk': self._checkmk_probe,
                    # RADIUS — AAA infrastructure proof.
                    'radius': self._radius_probe,
                    # SVNID — svnserve greeting.
                    'svnid': self._svn_probe,
                    # AMIID — Asterisk manager banner.
                    'amiid': self._ami_probe,
                    # TINCID — tinc mesh-VPN node name.
                    'tincid': self._tinc_probe,
                    # EPMDID — Erlang node names.
                    'epmdid': self._epmd_probe,
                    # JDWPID — Java debug agent exposed.
                    'jdwpid': self._jdwp_probe,
                    # PLEXID — Plex machine GUID.
                    'plexid': self._plex_probe,
                    # ADBID — Android device banner.
                    'adbid': self._adb_probe,
                })
            pool = [threading.Thread(target=_job, args=(n, f), daemon=True)
                    for n, f in jobs.items()]
            for t in pool:
                t.start()
            # One shared deadline for the whole wave — a fresh join(7.0) per
            # thread let every straggler stack another 7s on the total
            # (measured ~22s/IP under the 18-worker pool). The wave is meant
            # to cost ~max(probe): 12s covers the slowest job's own hard
            # timeout (the 4-echo ping subprocess, ~8s) plus headroom.
            _wave_deadline = time.time() + 12.0
            for t in pool:
                t.join(max(0.0, _wave_deadline - time.time()))

            # --- 2) GeoIP alternate (ipwho.is) ---
            alt = probes.get('alt_geoip')
            if alt is not None:
                acc, acity, aconn = alt
                org2 = f"{aconn.get('org','')} {aconn.get('isp','')}".strip()
                cast('AltGeoIP', acc, acity,
                     f"ipwho.is: {acity or '?'}, {acc or '?'}" + (f" · {org2}" if org2 else ""))
                if org2 and not report['org']:
                    report['org'] = org2

            # --- 3) Reverse DNS ---
            rd = probes.get('rdns')
            if rd is not None:
                rcc, rcity, rhost = rd
                report['rdns'] = rhost or ''
                if rcc or rhost:
                    cast('rDNS', rcc, rcity,
                         f"{rhost or 'no PTR'}" + (f" → {rcity or rcc}" if (rcc or rcity) else ""))

            # --- 3b) PTRX — forward-confirmed reverse DNS. A PTR whose own
            #         A/AAAA answers exclude the endpoint is a borrowed name
            #         (spoofed-identity mask — bulletproof hosts do this).
            px = probes.get('ptrx')
            if px:
                pst, phost = px
                report['ptrx'] = pst
                if pst == 'match':
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'PTRX', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'ok',
                         'detail': f"forward-confirmed rDNS — '{phost}' "
                                   f"resolves back to this IP"})
                elif pst == 'mismatch':
                    det = (f"rDNS '{phost}' does NOT resolve back to this "
                           f"IP — the PTR name is borrowed/forged "
                           f"(spoofed-identity mask)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('unconfirmed PTR name')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'PTRX', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                else:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'PTRX', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"rDNS '{phost}' failed to forward-resolve"})

            # --- 4) RDAP registry ---
            rdap_cc = probes.get('rdap')
            if rdap_cc:
                cast('RDAP', rdap_cc, '', f"registry country {rdap_cc}")

            # --- 5) RIPEstat: third geolocation + announced BGP prefix/origin AS
            #        + RPKI route-origin validity (a hijacked/misannounced
            #        prefix is a bulletproof-host / rogue-exit signature) ---
            rcc, rcity, rlat, rlon, rprefix, rasn, rpki = \
                probes.get('ripe') or ('', '', None, None, '', '', '')
            if rcc or rcity:
                cast('RIPE', rcc, rcity,
                     f"ripe.net: {rcity or '?'}, {rcc or '?'}"
                     + (f" ({rlat:.2f},{rlon:.2f})" if rlat and rlon else ""))
            if rprefix or rasn:
                report['ripe_prefix'] = rprefix
                report['ripe_asn'] = rasn
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'BGP', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                     'detail': f"announced {rprefix or '?'} via {rasn or '?'}"})
            if rpki:
                report['rpki_state'] = rpki
                if rpki == 'invalid':
                    det = (f"RPKI route-origin INVALID for "
                           f"{rprefix or 'prefix'} — announcement does not "
                           f"match the ROA (hijack / bulletproof-host "
                           f"signature)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('RPKI invalid — rogue announcement')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'RPKI', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                else:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'RPKI', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"RPKI route-origin {rpki}"
                                   + (f" for {rprefix}" if rprefix else "")})

            # --- 5b) Team Cymru DNS-ASN — second independent BGP source; also
            # yields the RIR and prefix allocation date. If the two BGP
            # sources name different origin ASes, that mismatch is itself
            # suspicious (prefix hijack / stale BGP data).
            cy = probes.get('cymru')
            if cy:
                cast('ASNDNS', cy['cc'], '',
                     f"cymru: {cy['asn']} {cy['prefix']} {cy['cc']} "
                     f"{cy['rir']} alloc {cy['allocated']}")
                if cy.get('prefix') and not report['ripe_prefix']:
                    report['ripe_prefix'] = cy['prefix']
                cy_asn = cy.get('asn', '')
                if (cy_asn and report.get('ripe_asn')
                        and cy_asn not in report['ripe_asn']):
                    det = (f"BGP sources disagree: RIPE {report['ripe_asn']} "
                           f"vs Cymru {cy_asn}")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('BGP origin-AS mismatch')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'ASNX', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                if cy_asn and not report['asn']:
                    report['asn'] = cy_asn
                if rasn and not report['asn']:
                    report['asn'] = rasn

            # --- 5c) GEOFEED — the operator's own declared location (RFC 9092).
            # Where GeoIP guesses and RDAP gives registry country, the geofeed
            # CSV is what the prefix holder SAYS about the block — the only
            # location source that is authoritative by declaration.
            gf = probes.get('geofeed')
            if gf:
                report['geofeed_cc'] = gf.get('cc', '')
                report['geofeed_city'] = gf.get('city', '')
                cast('GEOFEED', gf['cc'], gf.get('city', ''),
                     f"operator-declared {gf.get('city') or '?'},"
                     f"{gf['cc']} [{gf.get('prefix') or '?'} via {gf.get('rir') or 'rdap'}]")

            # --- 6) RTT latency band (active) ---
            rtt, rjit = probes.get('rtt') or (None, None)
            if rtt is not None:
                report['rtt_ms'] = rtt
                report['rtt_jitter_ms'] = rjit
                band = self._rtt_band(rtt)
                jit_s = f", jitter ±{rjit:.0f} ms" if rjit else ""
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'RTT', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                     'detail': f"avg {rtt:.0f} ms — {band}{jit_s}"})

            # --- 7) TCP-RTT divergence (active): the service's own RTT vs ICMP ---
            # If TCP completes at a very different distance than the IP's ICMP
            # path, the service is terminated somewhere else — a relay edge
            # closer than claimed, or a port-forward beyond the claimed host.
            tr = probes.get('tcp443')
            if tr is None:
                tr = probes.get('tcp80')
            if tr is not None:
                report['tcp_rtt_ms'] = tr
                ir = report.get('rtt_ms')
                # Asymmetric thresholds — a TCP path *shorter* than ICMP is the
                # interesting relay signal (service terminates at a closer edge);
                # TCP slower than ICMP is common on loaded/anycast hosts, so it
                # needs a much larger gap before it counts as evidence.
                closer = ir is not None and tr < ir * 0.55 and (ir - tr) > 20
                beyond = ir is not None and tr > ir * 2.2 and (tr - ir) > 60
                if closer or beyond:
                    direction = ('TCP terminates CLOSER than ICMP path'
                                 if closer else
                                 'TCP continues BEYOND the ICMP endpoint')
                    report['conflicts'].append(
                        f"TCP RTT {tr:.0f} ms vs ICMP {ir:.0f} ms — {direction}")
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'TCPRTT', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict',
                         'detail': f"tcp {tr:.0f} ms vs icmp {ir:.0f} ms — "
                                   f"{direction}"})
                else:
                    agree = (f", agrees with ICMP {ir:.0f} ms"
                             if ir is not None else "")
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'TCPRTT', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"tcp connect {tr:.0f} ms{agree}"})

            # --- 7b) TSRTT — passive TCP-handshake RTT harvested by the
            # sniffer (our SYN → remote SYN-ACK delta). This is a REAL measured
            # RTT for endpoints that filter ICMP, where ping goes silent — and
            # an independent cross-check when it doesn't. Feeds the PATH
            # ceiling below even when no ICMP answer ever arrives.
            if tsrtt_hint:
                report['tsrtt_ms'] = float(tsrtt_hint)
                _ir = report.get('rtt_ms')
                if _ir is None:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'TSRTT', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"passive handshake RTT {tsrtt_hint:.0f} ms — "
                                   f"ICMP unanswered; measured by the TCP "
                                   f"handshake itself"})
                else:
                    _div = abs(tsrtt_hint - _ir)
                    if _ir > 20 and tsrtt_hint < _ir * 0.4:
                        det = (f"passive TCP handshake RTT {tsrtt_hint:.0f} ms "
                               f"vs ICMP {_ir:.0f} ms — the flow completes far "
                               f"closer than the ICMP path: relay edge")
                        report['conflicts'].append(det)
                        report['labels'].append('handshake RTT diverges — relay')
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'TSRTT', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                             'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                    else:
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'TSRTT', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                             'status': 'info',
                             'detail': f"passive handshake RTT "
                                       f"{tsrtt_hint:.0f} ms"
                                       + (f", agrees with ICMP {_ir:.0f} ms"
                                          if _div <= max(15, _ir * 0.3)
                                          else f" vs ICMP {_ir:.0f} ms")})

            # --- 8) TTL / OS fingerprint (active) ---
            fp = probes.get('ttl')
            if fp is not None:
                report['ttl'] = fp['ttl']
                report['ttl_os'] = fp['os']
                report['hop_distance'] = fp['hops']
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'TTL/OS', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                     'detail': f"TTL {fp['ttl']} → initial {fp['initial']} "
                               f"(~{fp['hops']} hops, {fp['os']})"})

            # --- 9) HOPS last-mile geolocation (active, aims via TTL distance) ---
            # The upstream hops just short of the target reveal where the path
            # actually lands — the strongest passive check on a claimed location.
            mile = []
            for ttl, hip, hrtt in (probes.get('lastmile') or []):
                hg = {}
                if self._geoip is not None:
                    try:
                        hg = self._geoip.get_full(hip) or {}
                    except Exception:
                        hg = {}
                cc = (hg.get('countryCode') or hg.get('country') or '').upper()
                city = hg.get('city', '') or ''
                rdns = ''
                if not cc:
                    # GeoIP silent on this router — its PTR hostname is the
                    # fallback: backbone names carry IATA metro codes
                    # (cr01.seattle.wa.ibone…) or at least a ccTLD.
                    try:
                        rd = self._rdns_country(hip)
                        if rd:
                            cc, city, rdns = rd[0] or '', rd[1] or city, rd[2] or ''
                    except Exception:
                        pass
                mile.append({'hop': ttl, 'ip': hip, 'cc': cc,
                             'city': city, 'rdns': rdns, 'rtt': hrtt})
            if mile:
                report['last_mile'] = mile
                nearest = mile[-1]
                parts = ' · '.join(
                    f"hop{m['hop']} {m['ip']}→{m['city'] or '?'},"
                    f"{m['cc'] or '?'}" for m in mile)
                cast('HOPS', nearest['cc'], nearest['city'],
                     f"last-mile {parts}")

            # --- 9b) HOPDELTA — per-segment RTT deltas localise the last
            # mile. The delta between the furthest visible router and the
            # endpoint itself is the wire-distance of the last segment:
            # ~1-3 ms = same metro as its last router, ~10-30 ms = the IP's
            # real termination is a region/carrier hop BEYOND the router —
            # the last-mile stretch a relay's city claim can't fake.
            if mile and active:
                hops_r = sorted((m for m in mile if m.get('rtt') is not None),
                                key=lambda m: m['hop'])
                if hops_r:
                    ep_rtt = report.get('rtt_ms') or report.get('tsrtt_ms')
                    segs = []
                    prev = None
                    for m in hops_r:
                        if prev is not None and m['rtt'] is not None \
                                and prev['rtt'] is not None:
                            segs.append((prev['ip'], m['ip'],
                                         round(m['rtt'] - prev['rtt'], 1)))
                        prev = m
                    last_seg = None
                    if ep_rtt is not None and hops_r[-1]['rtt'] is not None:
                        last_seg = round(ep_rtt - hops_r[-1]['rtt'], 1)
                    hd = {'segments': segs, 'last_mile_ms': last_seg,
                          'last_router': hops_r[-1]['ip'],
                          'router_city': hops_r[-1].get('city', ''),
                          'router_cc': hops_r[-1].get('cc', '')}
                    report['hopdelta'] = hd
                    if last_seg is not None:
                        if last_seg <= 4.0:
                            det = (f"endpoint is {last_seg:.1f} ms beyond its "
                                   f"last visible router {hops_r[-1]['ip']} — "
                                   f"same metro as the router "
                                   f"({hops_r[-1].get('city') or '?'},"
                                   f"{hops_r[-1].get('cc') or '?'})")
                            cast('HOPDELTA', hops_r[-1].get('cc', ''),
                                 hops_r[-1].get('city', ''), det)
                        elif last_seg >= 12.0:
                            det = (f"endpoint is {last_seg:.1f} ms beyond its "
                                   f"last visible router — the real "
                                   f"termination sits a region/carrier hop "
                                   f"away from "
                                   f"{hops_r[-1].get('city') or '?'},"
                                   f"{hops_r[-1].get('cc') or '?'}")
                            report['methods'].append(
                                {'name': 'HOPDELTA', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                                 'status': 'info', 'detail': det})
                        else:
                            report['methods'].append(
                                {'name': 'HOPDELTA', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                                 'status': 'info',
                                 'detail': f"last-segment {last_seg:.1f} ms "
                                           f"past router {hops_r[-1]['ip']} — "
                                           f"same region"})

            # --- 10) VPN/proxy TCP port fingerprint (active) ---
            # SVCVAL first — TCP-accept proves nothing by itself: a transparent
            # L4 edge (Cloudflare Spectrum-class middleboxes, ISP proxies,
            # port-spoofing responders) completes ANY handshake. Each claimed
            # service is asked to speak its own protocol; only protocol-
            # confirmed ports keep evidence weight.
            open_ports = probes.get('ports') or []
            svcval = probes.get('svcval') or {}
            if open_ports and svcval:
                # PROX negotiation also counts as protocol confirmation.
                _px_ok = {p['port'] for p in (probes.get('prox') or [])
                          if p.get('state') in ('open', 'auth', 'answered')}
                confirmed = [(p, f"{l} [{svcval.get(p) or 'negotiated'}]", w)
                             for p, l, w in open_ports
                             if svcval.get(p) or p in _px_ok]
                unverified = [p for p, _l, _w in open_ports
                              if not svcval.get(p) and p not in _px_ok]
                if unverified:
                    report['accept_all_ports'] = sorted(unverified)
                report['svc_confirmed'] = [p for p, _l, _w in confirmed]
                if len(open_ports) >= 4 and not confirmed:
                    det = (f"{len(open_ports)} fingerprint ports accept TCP "
                           f"but NONE answer their protocol — indiscriminate "
                           f"accept signature: transparent L4 edge/middlebox "
                           f"relay (Spectrum-class) or a port-spoofing "
                           f"responder; port evidence is unverifiable")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append(
                        'transparent TCP accept-all edge (L4 relay)')
                    report['methods'].append({
                        'name': 'ACCALL', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                        'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                    open_ports = []
                elif confirmed or unverified:
                    open_ports = confirmed + [
                        (p, l + ' (TCP-accept only)', 0)
                        for p, l, _w in open_ports if p in set(unverified)]
                if confirmed:
                    report['methods'].append({
                        'name': 'SVCVAL', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                        'status': 'conflict' if any(w >= 35
                                                    for _p, _l, w in confirmed)
                                      else 'info',
                        'detail': 'protocol-confirmed services: ' + ', '.join(
                            f"{p}/{svcval.get(p) or 'negotiated'}"
                            for p, _l, _w in confirmed)})
            report['open_ports'] = open_ports
            if open_ports:
                pretty = ", ".join(f"{p} {lbl}" for p, lbl, _ in open_ports)
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'PortFP', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                     'detail': f"open service ports: {pretty}"})

            # --- 11) Infrastructure classification ---
            self._classify_infra(ip, geo, report)

            # --- 11b) Tor exit check — the official TorDNSEL exit list names
            # known anonymizer egress directly: one DNS query to OUR resolver,
            # zero contact with the endpoint. A positive answer is definitive
            # proof the IP is shared anonymity infrastructure — stronger than
            # any heuristic score, and the hardest "tunnel" there is.
            tor_ans = probes.get('tor')
            if tor_ans:
                report['is_tor'] = True
                report['is_proxy'] = True
                det = (f"Tor exit node (DNSEL {tor_ans}) — officially listed "
                       f"anonymizer egress; the IP is shared anonymity infra")
                report['conflicts'].append(det)
                report['labels'].append('Tor exit node (DNSEL confirmed)')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'TOREXIT', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})

            # --- 11c) EXITLIST — provider-published exit identity. The VPN
            # operator's OWN server catalogue naming this IP is the
            # registry-class truth about which anonymizer it belongs to,
            # including the exit's real city — authoritative, not guessed.
            ent, apple = probes.get('exit') or (None, None)
            if ent:
                report['exit_provider'] = ent.get('provider', '')
                report['exit_kind'] = ent.get('kind', '')
                report['exit_host'] = ent.get('host', '')
                report['exit_city'] = ent.get('city', '')
                report['exit_cc'] = ent.get('cc', '')
                report['is_vpn'] = True
                det = (f"{ent.get('provider','?')} published exit "
                       f"{ent.get('host') or ip} — operator's own server "
                       f"list places it in "
                       f"{ent.get('city') or '?'},{ent.get('cc') or '?'}")
                report['conflicts'].append(det)
                report['labels'].append(
                    f"{ent.get('provider','?')} exit (provider list)")
                cast('EXITLIST', ent.get('cc', ''), ent.get('city', ''), det)
            if apple:
                report['apple_relay'] = 'egress'
                report['apple_cc'] = apple.get('cc', '')
                report['apple_city'] = apple.get('city', '')
                report['apple_region'] = apple.get('region', '')
                report['is_vpn'] = True
                report['is_proxy'] = True
                det = (f"Apple iCloud Private Relay egress "
                       f"({apple.get('city') or '?'},{apple.get('cc') or '?'}) "
                       f"— Apple's relay chooses an egress NEAR the user, "
                       f"so the user is approximately in/near this city; "
                       f"the IP is a relay mask, not the subscriber")
                report['conflicts'].append(det)
                report['labels'].append(
                    f"Apple Private Relay egress ({apple.get('city') or '?'})")
                cast('APPLERLY', apple.get('cc', ''), apple.get('city', ''),
                     det)

            # --- 11d) ONIONOO — Tor's official relay directory. Catches
            # guard/middle relays TorDNSEL (exit-only) misses, and returns
            # the relay's nickname + flags.
            oo = probes.get('onionoo')
            if oo:
                report['onionoo'] = dict(oo)
                report['is_tor'] = True
                report['is_proxy'] = True
                role = 'exit' if oo.get('exit') else 'relay'
                det = (f"Tor {role} '{oo.get('host') or '?'}' "
                       f"fp:{oo.get('fp','?')} flags={oo.get('flags') or []} "
                       f"— Tor Project directory record")
                if not tor_ans:
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append(f'Tor {role} node (onionoo)')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'ONIONOO', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})

            # --- 11e) DNSBL — community-maintained abuse/exit blocklists.
            # A listing names the shared/anonymising reputation class.
            db = probes.get('dnsbl') or []
            if db:
                report['dnsbl'] = list(db)
                det = ('DNSBL listings: ' + '; '.join(db)
                       + ' — reputation-classified shared/anonymising IP')
                report['labels'].append('DNSBL-listed address')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'DNSBL', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'info', 'detail': det})

            # --- 11f) CRTSH — certificate-transparency identity. Every cert
            # ever logged under the endpoint's claimed rDNS domain names the
            # real operator — the cert archive outlives a rotating live cert.
            cs = probes.get('crtsh')
            if cs:
                report['crtsh'] = dict(cs)
                vpn_kw = next((kw for kw in _VPN_ORG_MARKERS
                               if any(kw in n for n in cs.get('names', []))),
                              None)
                det = (f"crt.sh: {cs.get('certs',0)} logged certs under "
                       f"{cs.get('domain','?')}: "
                       + ', '.join(cs.get('names', [])[:4]))
                if vpn_kw:
                    det += f" — '{vpn_kw.strip()}' names VPN infrastructure"
                    report['is_vpn'] = True
                    report['labels'].append(
                        f"VPN domain in cert transparency ({vpn_kw.strip()})")
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'CRTSH', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict' if vpn_kw else 'info',
                     'detail': det})

            # --- 12) DNS leak / resolver mismatch ---
            dns_detail, dns_cc = self._dns_leak_check(ip)
            if dns_detail:
                report['dns_leak'] = dns_detail
                if dns_cc:
                    cast('DNSLeak', dns_cc, '', dns_detail)
                else:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'DNSLeak', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': dns_detail})

            # --- 13) TLS JA3 fingerprint (active, port 443 only) ---
            ja3_hash, ja3_detail, cert, tls_hdrs, h2 = \
                probes.get('tls') or (None, None, None, None, None)
            if ja3_hash:
                report['tls_ja3'] = ja3_hash
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'TLS/JA3', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                     'detail': ja3_detail})

            # --- 14) TLS server certificate identity (shares the :443 handshake) —
            # the cert names the real operator (VPN provider, CDN, org) and its
            # SAN hostnames can carry the same city-code hints as rDNS.
            if cert:
                report['tls_cert_cn'] = cert.get('cn', '')
                report['tls_cert_org'] = cert.get('org', '')
                report['tls_cert_sans'] = cert.get('sans', [])
                cdetail = (f"CN={cert.get('cn') or '?'} · O={cert.get('org') or '?'}"
                           f" · iss={cert.get('issuer') or '?'}"
                           f" · sans={len(cert.get('sans', []))}")
                cblob = ' '.join(str(x) for x in
                                 (cert.get('cn', ''), cert.get('org', ''),
                                  cert.get('issuer', ''), *cert.get('sans', []))).lower()
                for kw in _VPN_ORG_MARKERS:
                    if kw in cblob:
                        report['is_vpn'] = True
                        report['labels'].append(
                            f"VPN/anon name '{kw.strip()}' (TLS cert)")
                        break
                else:
                    for kw in _HOSTING_ORG_MARKERS:
                        if kw in cblob:
                            report['is_hosting'] = True
                            report['labels'].append(
                                f"hosting/datacenter ({kw.strip()}, TLS cert)")
                            break
                san_cc, san_city = '', ''
                for san in cert.get('sans', []):
                    sm = _RDNS_CODE_RE.search(str(san).lower())
                    if sm:
                        san_city, san_cc = _RDNS_CITY_CODES[sm.group(1).lower()]
                        cdetail += f" · SAN {san}={san_city},{san_cc}"
                        break
                cast('CERT', san_cc, san_city, cdetail)

            # --- 14b) H2FP — HTTP/2 SETTINGS fingerprint. When ALPN negotiated
            # h2, the server's answering SETTINGS frame fingerprints its
            # service stack (nginx/envoy/CF/http2.sys all differ) — a second
            # provenance signal on top of TLS parameters and the cert.
            if h2 and h2.get('alpn') == 'h2' and h2.get('sig'):
                report['h2_alpn'] = 'h2'
                report['h2_sig'] = h2['sig']
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'H2FP', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                     'detail': f"h2 settings {h2['sig']}"
                               + (f" · mcs={h2['mcs']} iw={h2['iw']}"
                                  if h2.get('mcs') else "")})

            # --- 15) MSS clamping (packet-layer, harvested from the sniffer) —
            # the endpoint's advertised TCP MSS should be ~1460 on a plain
            # Ethernet path; VPN tunnels, PPPoE and IPsec clamps shrink it.
            # Values ≤1380 are strong evidence of encapsulation on the path.
            if mss_hint:
                report['mss'] = int(mss_hint)
                mss = int(mss_hint)
                if mss <= 1320:
                    status = 'conflict'
                    det = (f"MSS {mss} — heavy clamping (≥140B below Ethernet "
                           f"1460): tunnel/PPPoE encapsulation on path")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('MSS clamped — tunnel on path')
                elif mss <= 1380:
                    status = 'conflict'
                    det = (f"MSS {mss} — clamped below Ethernet norm "
                           f"(encapsulating hop: VPN tunnel / PPPoE / IPsec)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('MSS clamped — tunnel on path')
                else:
                    status = 'info'
                    det = f"MSS {mss} — unclamped Ethernet path"
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'MSS', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': status,
                     'detail': det})

            # --- 16) PMTU — DF-flagged ping ladder measures the real path MTU.
            # Anything below 1500 means bytes of encapsulation overhead are
            # being deducted by a hop on the path — ~1420 is the WireGuard
            # signature, ~1492 PPPoE, lower still means stacked tunnels.
            mtu = probes.get('pmtu')
            if mtu:
                report['path_mtu'] = mtu
                overhead = 1500 - mtu
                if mtu >= 1500:
                    mst, mdet = 'info', "path MTU 1500 — no encapsulation detected"
                elif mtu >= 1420:
                    mst = 'conflict'
                    mdet = (f"path MTU ~{mtu} — ~{overhead}B tunnel overhead "
                            f"(WireGuard-class encapsulation / PPPoE)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(mdet)
                    report['labels'].append('path MTU reduced — tunnel on path')
                else:
                    mst = 'conflict'
                    mdet = (f"path MTU ~{mtu} — ~{overhead}B encapsulation "
                            f"overhead (heavy / stacked tunnels)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(mdet)
                    report['labels'].append('path MTU reduced — tunnel on path')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'PMTU', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': mst,
                     'detail': mdet})

            # --- 17) TCPFP — passive TCP fingerprint cross-check. The sniffer
            # captured the remote's SYN/SYN-ACK window+options+TTL; its derived
            # OS class must agree with the ping TTL method. A Windows-vs-unix
            # split means a middlebox/NAT is answering on the path.
            if tcpfp_hint:
                fp_os = self._os_from_tcpfp(tcpfp_hint)
                report['tcpfp_os'] = fp_os
                report['tcpfp_win'] = tcpfp_hint.get('win', 0)
                report['tcpfp_opts'] = tcpfp_hint.get('opts', '')
                ping_os = (report.get('ttl_os') or '').lower()
                fpos = fp_os.lower()
                win_side = 'windows' in fpos
                unix_side = not win_side and 'net-device' not in fpos
                p_win = 'windows' in ping_os
                p_unix = bool(ping_os) and not p_win
                if fp_os and ping_os and ((win_side and p_unix) or (unix_side and p_win)):
                    det = (f"passive TCP fp → {fp_os} (win={report['tcpfp_win']}, "
                           f"ttl={tcpfp_hint.get('ttl')}) vs ping TTL → "
                           f"{report['ttl_os']} — OS classes disagree: "
                           f"middlebox/NAT on path")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('TCP fingerprint OS mismatch')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'TCPFP', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                elif fp_os:
                    agree = ("agrees with" if ping_os and
                             not ((win_side and p_unix) or (unix_side and p_win))
                             else "unresolved vs")
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'TCPFP', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"passive TCP fp → {fp_os} "
                                   f"(win={report['tcpfp_win']}, "
                                   f"opts={report['tcpfp_opts'][:40]}) — "
                                   f"{agree} TTL/OS {report.get('ttl_os') or '?'}"})
                # TCP-vs-ICMP hop divergence — the sniffed SYN-ACK's TTL yields
                # the TCP path's hop count; the ping yields ICMP's. A ≥3-hop
                # split means the two traffic classes terminate on different
                # infrastructure — a relay/redirect signature.
                fp_ttl = tcpfp_hint.get('ttl') or 0
                ping_hops = report.get('hop_distance')
                if fp_ttl and ping_hops is not None:
                    fp_init = 64 if fp_ttl <= 64 else (128 if fp_ttl <= 128 else 255)
                    hops_tcp = fp_init - fp_ttl
                    report['tcp_hops'] = hops_tcp
                    if abs(hops_tcp - ping_hops) >= 3:
                        det = (f"TCP path ~{hops_tcp} hops vs ICMP "
                               f"~{ping_hops} — traffic classes terminate on "
                               f"different infrastructure (relay/redirect)")
                        report['conflicts'].append(det)
                        report['labels'].append('TCP/ICMP path split')
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'TTLX', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                             'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                    else:
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'TTLX', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                             'status': 'info',
                             'detail': f"TCP ~{hops_tcp} hops vs ICMP "
                                       f"~{ping_hops} — same termination"})

            # --- 17b) TSCLK — TCP timestamp clock profile. Every SYN-ACK the
            # sniffer saw carries the remote's TSval: a monotonic counter
            # ticking at the answering stack's clock rate (~1000Hz Linux/BSD,
            # lower on Windows). Between samples, ONE physical clock can only
            # advance at its own rate — a backwards jump or an impossible
            # slope means a different stack answered that SYN, so several
            # machines sit behind the address: the "single endpoint" story is
            # false — shared-egress / load-balanced infrastructure, which is
            # what a VPN concentrator is. Also yields an uptime estimate
            # (tsval/rate): freshly rotated cloud exits read in hours.
            if tsclk_hint:
                _s = sorted((w, int(t)) for w, t in tsclk_hint if t)
                _rates, _mux = [], 0
                for (w1, t1), (w2, t2) in zip(_s, _s[1:]):
                    dt = w2 - w1
                    if dt < 0.25:
                        continue
                    d = t2 - t1
                    if d < 0 or d / dt > 20000:
                        _mux += 1
                    else:
                        _rates.append(d / dt)
                hz = sum(_rates) / len(_rates) if _rates else 0.0
                if hz:
                    report['tsclk_hz'] = round(hz)
                    report['tsclk_uptime_s'] = int(_s[-1][1] / hz)
                # Count distinct clocks, not violations: with the measured rate,
                # each sample's implied base (tsval - hz*wall) identifies WHICH
                # stack answered. Alternating A,B,A,B is 2 streams, not 4.
                _streams = 1
                if _mux and hz:
                    _bases = sorted(t - hz * w for w, t in _s)
                    _tol = hz * 30          # two bases ~30s of ticks apart = one clock
                    _streams = 1 + sum(1 for b1, b2 in zip(_bases, _bases[1:])
                                       if b2 - b1 > _tol)
                report['tsclk_streams'] = (_streams if hz else 2) if _mux else 1
                report['tsclk_mux'] = _mux > 0
                if _s:
                    _up = ''
                    if report['tsclk_uptime_s']:
                        _u = report['tsclk_uptime_s']
                        _up = (f", uptime ~{_u/86400:.1f}d" if _u >= 86400
                               else f", uptime ~{_u/3600:.1f}h")
                    _clk = f"~{hz:.0f}Hz" if hz else "?Hz"
                    if _mux:
                        det = (f"{report['tsclk_streams']} independent TCP clock "
                               f"streams on one IP ({_mux} clock discontinuities "
                               f"in {len(_s)} SYN-ACK samples, {_clk}{_up}) — "
                               f"several physical stacks answer behind this "
                               f"address (shared egress / LB front)")
                        report['conflicts'].append(det)
                        report['labels'].append(
                            'multiplexed endpoint — multiple TCP clocks')
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'TSCLK', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                             'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                    else:
                        _oscls = ('unix-class clock' if hz >= 500 else
                                  ('windows-class clock' if hz else ''))
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'TSCLK', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                             'status': 'info',
                             'detail': f"single TCP clock {_clk}"
                                       + (f" ({_oscls})" if _oscls else '')
                                       + f"{_up} — {len(_s)} sample(s), one stack"})

            # --- 17c) IPID — passive IP-ID stream analysis. The sniffer records
            # the IP header's ID field on inbound packets; consecutive IDs from
            # ONE stack advance along one counter (or sit at 0 — the Linux DF
            # convention). Multiple disjoint counter families interleaved on one
            # source IP means several stacks answer behind the address — the IP-
            # layer version of the TSCLK multiplex proof, working even when the
            # endpoint negotiates no TCP timestamp option. Corroborator only.
            if ipid_hint:
                _ip = self._ipid_analyze(ipid_hint)
                report['ipid_kind'] = _ip.get('kind', '')
                report['ipid_streams'] = _ip.get('streams', 0)
                if _ip.get('streams', 0) >= 2:
                    det = (f"{_ip['streams']} disjoint IP-ID counter streams in "
                           f"{_ip['n']} sniffed packets — several stacks share "
                           f"this address (shared egress / NAT front)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('multiplexed endpoint — IP-ID streams')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'IPID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                elif _ip.get('kind'):
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'IPID', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"IP-ID {_ip['kind']} "
                                   f"({_ip['n']} sniffed packets, one stream)"})

            # --- 18) TUNX tunnel localization — MSS is what the REMOTE
            # advertises, PMTU is what the PATH measured. Crossed, they say
            # where the encapsulation sits: clamped MSS + clean PMTU means the
            # remote's own link tunnels (its VPN/PPPoE); clean MSS + low PMTU
            # means the overhead is on the near path (our tunnel/ISP/in-path
            # relay), not at the endpoint.
            mss_v, mtu_v = report.get('mss'), report.get('path_mtu')
            if mss_v or mtu_v:
                det = st = None
                if mtu_v and mtu_v >= 1500 and mss_v and mss_v <= 1380:
                    st = 'conflict'
                    det = (f"remote MSS {mss_v} clamped but path MTU is clean "
                           f"— encapsulation is on the ENDPOINT's link "
                           f"(its own VPN/PPPoE), not in between")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('remote-side tunnel (endpoint link)')
                elif mtu_v and mtu_v < 1500 and (not mss_v or mss_v >= 1460):
                    st = 'info'
                    det = (f"path MTU ~{mtu_v} but remote MSS "
                           f"{mss_v or '?'} is unclamped — overhead is on the "
                           f"near path (local tunnel/ISP), not at the endpoint")
                elif mtu_v and mtu_v < 1500 and mss_v and mss_v <= 1380:
                    st = 'conflict'
                    det = (f"path MTU ~{mtu_v} + remote MSS {mss_v} both "
                           f"clamped — encapsulation confirmed end-to-end")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('tunnel confirmed (PMTU+MSS agree)')
                if det:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'TUNX', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': st,
                         'detail': det})

            # --- 19) EDGE — HTTP response headers on the service port.
            # `Via:` literally names an in-path HTTP proxy (relay proof).
            # CF-RAY / x-amz-cf-pop / X-Served-By carry IATA codes for the
            # edge datacenter that actually answered — the true serving
            # location for anycast/CDN endpoints whose GeoIP is ambiguous.
            # The :443 TLS handshake answered an HTTP GET too, so its headers
            # fill in when port 80 is closed or silent.
            hb = probes.get('http')
            if tls_hdrs:
                if hb:
                    for k in ('server', 'via', 'edge_city', 'edge_cc',
                              'edge_iata', 'edge_src', 'status', 'location',
                              'redirect_to', 'redirect_server',
                              'redirect_edge_city', 'redirect_edge_cc',
                              'redirect_edge_iata'):
                        if not hb.get(k) and tls_hdrs.get(k):
                            hb[k] = tls_hdrs[k]
                else:
                    hb = tls_hdrs
            if hb:
                report['http_server'] = hb.get('server', '')
                report['http_via'] = hb.get('via', '')
                if hb.get('edge_city'):
                    report['edge_city'] = hb['edge_city']
                    report['edge_cc'] = hb.get('edge_cc', '')
                    report['edge_iata'] = hb.get('edge_iata', '')
                    cast('EDGE', report['edge_cc'], report['edge_city'],
                         f"edge PoP {hb.get('edge_src','')}={hb.get('edge_iata')} → "
                         f"{report['edge_city']},{report['edge_cc']}")
                if hb.get('via'):
                    det = (f"HTTP Via: {hb['via'][:60]} — an in-path proxy "
                           f"identifies itself in the response")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('in-path HTTP proxy (Via header)')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'VIA', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'conflict',
                         'detail': det})
                elif hb.get('server'):
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'HTTP', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'info',
                         'detail': f"HTTP server: {hb['server'][:60]}"})
                # RDRCT — the redirect target is evidence itself: where the
                # service wants to send us, and (when chased) which edge PoP
                # the landing landed on. A landing edge that disagrees with
                # the first hop's PoP shows the front/landing topology.
                rloc = hb.get('redirect_to', '')
                if rloc:
                    report['rdrct'] = rloc
                    rcc = hb.get('redirect_edge_cc', '')
                    rcity = hb.get('redirect_edge_city', '')
                    if rcity and report.get('edge_city') and \
                            rcity != report['edge_city']:
                        det = (f"HTTP {hb.get('redirect_status') or '3xx'} → "
                               f"{rloc[:70]} — landing edge {rcity},{rcc} "
                               f"differs from first-hop "
                               f"{report['edge_city']},{report['edge_cc']}")
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'RDRCT', 'cc': rcc, 'city': rcity,
                             'status': 'info', 'detail': det})
                    else:
                        report['methods'].append(
                            {'name': 'RDRCT', 'cc': rcc, 'city': rcity,
                             'status': 'info',
                             'detail': f"redirect {hb.get('redirect_status') or '3xx'}"
                                       f" → {rloc[:80]}"
                                       + (f" (lands {rcity},{rcc})"
                                          if rcity else "")})

            # --- 19b) IKE — one IKEv2 SA_INIT to UDP 500 (NAT-T 4500 fallback).
            # A formatted IKE response is PROTOCOL-LEVEL proof the endpoint runs
            # an IPsec responder — a VPN gateway answering in its own protocol.
            # Stronger than every port heuristic: the service itself identifies.
            ike = probes.get('ike')
            if ike:
                report['ike_responder'] = True
                report['ike_vendor'] = ike.get('vendor', '')
                vd = f" · {ike['vendor']}" if ike.get('vendor') else ''
                nt = (f" ({', '.join(ike['notifies'])})" if ike.get('notifies') else '')
                det = (f"IKE{ike.get('version','')} responder on udp/{ike.get('port',500)}"
                       f"{vd}{nt} — endpoint IS an IPsec VPN gateway")
                report['ike_detail'] = det
                report['is_vpn'] = True
                report['conflicts'].append(det)
                report['labels'].append('IKE/IPsec VPN gateway (protocol confirmed)')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'IKE', 'cc': '', 'city': '', 'status': 'conflict',
                     'detail': det})

            # --- 19b-i) IKEV1 — legacy aggressive-mode. Modern stacks ignore
            # it; a responder is a legacy IPsec gateway, and its reply's ID
            # payload declares its own identity (FQDN/IP) in cleartext.
            ik1 = probes.get('ike1')
            if ik1 and ik1.get('answered'):
                report['ike1'] = dict(ik1)
                rid = ik1.get('id') or ''
                det = (f"IKEv1 responder on udp/500 — legacy IPsec gateway"
                       + (f" self-declares ID '{rid}'" if rid else
                          " (no ID payload disclosed)"))
                report['is_vpn'] = True
                report['conflicts'].append(det)
                report['labels'].append(
                    'IKEv1 legacy IPsec gateway'
                    + (f' ({rid})' if rid else ''))
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'IKEV1', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})

            # --- 19b-ii) PORTAL — HTTPS VPN portals hide every tunnel behind
            # one :443 socket, but their unauthenticated pages still name
            # the product family. A hit here upgrades "TLS endpoint" to a
            # named VPN portal.
            port_hits = probes.get('portal') or []
            if port_hits:
                report['portal'] = port_hits
                report['is_vpn'] = True
                det = ' · '.join(f"{h['label']} ({h['path']})"
                                 for h in port_hits[:3])
                report['conflicts'].append(
                    f"VPN portal identified over HTTPS: {det}")
                report['labels'].append(
                    f"VPN portal: {port_hits[0]['kind']}")
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'PORTAL', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})

            # --- 19b-iii) IDENT — the self-naming services. Whatever the IP
            # claims to be, a NetBIOS node-status, SNMP sysName, NTP refid,
            # SSDP server banner or SQL-browser answer is the endpoint
            # stating its own identity — the strongest single packet of
            # ground truth a consumer box or misconfigured server can emit.
            idn = probes.get('ident') or {}
            if idn:
                report['ident'] = dict(idn)
                frag = []
                if idn.get('nbns_names'):
                    frag.append('NBNS ' + '/'.join(idn['nbns_names'][:4])
                                + (f" MAC {idn['nbns_mac']}"
                                   f" ({idn.get('nbns_vendor','?')})"
                                   if idn.get('nbns_mac') else ''))
                if idn.get('snmp'):
                    frag.append('SNMP ' + '; '.join(idn['snmp'][:2]))
                if idn.get('ntp'):
                    n = idn['ntp']
                    frag.append(
                        f"NTP stratum {n['stratum']}"
                        + (f" ← upstream {n['refid_ip']}"
                           if n.get('refid_ip') else
                           f" ref {n.get('refid','?')}"))
                if idn.get('ssdp'):
                    frag.append('SSDP ' + (idn['ssdp'].get('server') or
                                           idn['ssdp'].get('location') or ''))
                if idn.get('ssrp'):
                    s = idn['ssrp']
                    frag.append(f"SQL-Browser {s.get('name','?')}"
                                f"\\{s.get('instance','?')}"
                                f" v{s.get('version','?')}")
                # The NTP upstream source is a corroborating alias of the
                # same network — queue it as a related endpoint.
                nref = (idn.get('ntp') or {}).get('refid_ip') or ''
                if nref and _is_public_ip_cached(nref):
                    report.setdefault('_alias_hints', []).append(
                        ('ntp-upstream', nref))
                det = ('self-naming services answered: ' + '; '.join(frag)
                       + ' — the endpoint states its own identity')
                report['conflicts'].append(det)
                report['labels'].append('self-naming endpoint service(s)')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'IDENT', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})

            # --- 19b-iv) CHAOS — the endpoint's own DNS service telling you
            # its name/version, plus an open-recursion finding.
            ch = probes.get('chaos') or {}
            if ch:
                report['chaos'] = dict(ch)
                frag = []
                if ch.get('hostname'):
                    frag.append(f"hostname.bind → '{ch['hostname']}'")
                if ch.get('version'):
                    frag.append(f"version.bind → '{ch['version']}'")
                if ch.get('id'):
                    frag.append(f"id.server → '{ch['id']}'")
                if ch.get('recursion'):
                    frag.append('recurses for strangers (open resolver — '
                                'a DNS-tunnel hop anyone can use)')
                    report['labels'].append('open DNS resolver')
                det = 'DNS CHAOS: ' + '; '.join(frag)
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'CHAOS', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict' if ch.get('recursion') else 'info',
                     'detail': det})
                if ch.get('recursion'):
                    report['conflicts'].append(
                        'open recursive resolver — usable as a free '
                        'third-party relay / amplification hop')

            # --- 19b-v) STUNP — an active reflexive relay is proven by its
            # Binding Response; SOFTWARE names the stack (coturn…).
            st = probes.get('stunp') or {}
            if st.get('answered'):
                report['stun_srv'] = dict(st)
                det = ('STUN Binding Response on udp/3478 — endpoint runs a '
                       'reflexive relay'
                       + (f" ({st['software']})" if st.get('software') else '')
                       + ' — WebRTC/VoIP-class tunnel transport')
                report['labels'].append('STUN/TURN reflexive relay')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'STUNP', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                report['conflicts'].append(det)

            # --- 19b-iv) SMBID — the SMB NTLMSSP challenge volunteers the
            # endpoint's own machine identity in cleartext before any auth:
            # NetBIOS name, DNS domain, DNS hostname, and the server clock.
            # The mask hides the address, never the machine answering it.
            sm = probes.get('smbid')
            if sm:
                report['smbid'] = dict(sm)
                if sm.get('dns_host') or sm.get('name'):
                    who = (sm.get('dns_host') or '').strip() \
                        or (sm.get('name') or '').strip()
                    dom = (sm.get('dns_domain') or sm.get('nb_domain')
                           or '').strip()
                    det = (f"SMB endpoint self-identified: host '{who}'"
                           + (f" domain '{dom}'" if dom else "")
                           + (f" clock skew {sm['clock_skew_s']:+.0f}s"
                              if sm.get('clock_skew_s') is not None else '')
                           + " — the machine answered its real name "
                             "pre-auth (no mask strips it)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('SMB self-identified host')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'SMBID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                else:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'SMBID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'info',
                         'detail': 'SMB/NTLMSSP answered on 445/139 — '
                                   'Windows-class endpoint (no identity AV '
                                   'pairs returned)'})

            # --- 19b-v) ICMPTS — the ICMP timestamp reply carries the
            # endpoint's own millisecond-of-day. Spec-conforming hosts
            # answer UTC (delta ≈ 0); most answering gear answers LOCAL
            # time — the delta is the endpoint's UTC offset, a timezone
            # proof the GeoIP claim can't fake.
            its = probes.get('icmpts')
            if its:
                report['icmpts'] = dict(its)
                if its.get('remote_ms') is not None:
                    off = its.get('implied_utc_offset_h')
                    _tzdet = (f" implies UTC{off:+g} — endpoint's clock reads "
                              f"local time" if off and abs(off) >= 0.5 else
                              " — clock reads UTC")
                    det = (f"ICMP timestamp answered: remote ms-of-day "
                           f"{its['remote_ms']} (delta {its['delta_s']}s){_tzdet}"
                           " — router-class gear responds to type-13")
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'ICMPTS', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict' if off and abs(off) >= 0.5
                         else 'info', 'detail': det})
                    if off and abs(off) >= 0.5:
                        report['labels'].append(
                            f"ICMP clock says UTC{off:+g}")
                else:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'ICMPTS', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'info',
                         'detail': 'ICMP timestamp-reply (type 14) answered — '
                                  'router-class endpoint'})

            # --- 19b-vi) LDAPID — the rootDSE reply carries the domain in
            # cleartext: namingContexts 'DC=corp,DC=local' and dnsHostName
            # 'dc01.corp.local' ARE the AD identity the address hid.
            ld = probes.get('ldapid')
            if ld:
                report['ldapid'] = dict(ld)
                who = (ld.get('dnsHostName') or ld.get('serverName')
                       or '').strip()
                dom = (ld.get('defaultNamingContext')
                       or (ld.get('namingContexts') or '').split(',')[0:1])
                dom = (dom[0] if isinstance(dom, list) and dom
                       else (dom or '')).strip()
                if who or dom:
                    det = (f"LDAP rootDSE self-identified:"
                           + (f" host '{who}'" if who else '')
                           + (f" context '{dom}'" if dom else '')
                           + " — the endpoint's directory identity in "
                             "cleartext (anonymous bind, no auth)")
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)
                    report['labels'].append('LDAP self-identified host')
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'LDAPID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'conflict', 'detail': det})
                else:
                    report['methods'].append(
                        {'name': 'LDAPID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                         'status': 'info',
                         'detail': 'LDAP rootDSE answered on tcp/389 — '
                                   'directory service endpoint'})

            # --- 19b-vii) RTSPID — the RTSP Server: banner names the
            # vendor+firmware of the camera/NVR/media-relay behind the
            # forward: embedded-device class, proven by its own string.
            rt = probes.get('rtspid')
            if rt:
                report['rtspid'] = dict(rt)
                srv = (rt.get('server') or '').strip()
                det = (f"RTSP answered ({rt.get('status') or '?'})"
                       + (f" — Server: {srv}" if srv else '')
                       + " — camera/NVR/media-relay class endpoint")
                report['labels'].append('RTSP media endpoint')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'RTSPID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'conflict' if srv else 'info',
                     'detail': det})
                if srv:
                    report['conflicts'].append(det)

            # --- 19b-viii) RDPID — the X.224 negotiation response declares
            # the security stack: an RDP host proven by its own protocol,
            # and NLA mode read off the wire.
            rp = probes.get('rdpid')
            if rp:
                report['rdpid'] = dict(rp)
                _prot = rp.get('protocols') or []
                det = (f"RDP negotiation answered — security stack "
                       f"{'+'.join(_prot) if _prot else 'legacy'}"
                       + (" (NLA required)" if rp.get('nla') else "")
                       + (f" [failure 0x{rp['failure']:x}]"
                          if rp.get('failure') is not None else "")
                       + " — Windows remote-desktop service proven")
                report['labels'].append('RDP endpoint')
                report['methods'].append(
                    {'name': 'RDPID', 'cc': '', 'city': '',
                     'status': 'info', 'detail': det})

            # --- 19b-ix) SVCID — an open self-naming service names the
